In the Han–Three Kingdoms institutional context, **“录尚书事” (lu shangshu shi)** — often translated “Recorder/Overseer of the Imperial Secretariat” — meant that Zhuge Liang was the high minister charged with **supervising the Imperial Secretariat (尚书台)**. The “录” here meant to oversee or take charge of, not merely to keep records.

The **Imperial Secretariat** was the central document-processing organ of the government. It handled the flow of:

- memorials and reports from officials to the throne;
- edicts, commands, and policy decisions from the throne to the bureaucracy;
- personnel matters such as appointments, promotions, rewards, and punishments;
- routine state administration: finance and grain, law, rituals, military reports and logistics, and other government business.

Its subordinate sections (曹) dealt with different categories of affairs. As **录尚书事**, Zhuge Liang participated in and supervised this whole apparatus: he reviewed and directed the business of the Secretariat, supervised the **尚书令** and other Secretariat officials, and coordinated the various sections. In practice, this made him master of the regular central civil administration.

Chen Shou’s *Sanguozhi* records that Liu Bei made Zhuge Liang **丞相** and **录尚书事**, and after Liu Shan succeeded, “政事无巨细，咸决于亮” — all government affairs, great and small, were decided by Zhuge Liang. So the charge chiefly meant **oversight of the Imperial Secretariat’s handling of memorials, edicts, personnel, and day-to-day state administration** — that is, control of the central civil government, not a mere clerical or recording duty.
